From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mathieu GRENET <mathieu@mgth.fr>
Date: Tue, 26 May 2026 15:32:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 04/28] ao/asio: fix --asio-* option prefix in error message
 and comment

The driver name is `asio`, and the m_option entries use
`.options_prefix = "asio"`, so the public option names are
`--asio-device`, `--asio-sample-type`, etc. (mirroring how
ao_wasapi exposes `--wasapi-exclusive-buffer`).

Two strings in ao_asio.c incorrectly referred to these options as
`--ao-asio-*`:

- The MP_ERR message printed when --asio-sample-type forces a
  format incompatible with the driver's native sample type told
  the user to look at `--ao-asio-sample-type`, which mpv would
  reject as "option not found".
- A nearby comment used the same incorrect spelling.

Replace both with the correct `--asio-*` prefix.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 audio/out/ao_asio.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/audio/out/ao_asio.c b/audio/out/ao_asio.c
index 5087ce96f3..3a78abd020 100644
--- a/audio/out/ao_asio.c
+++ b/audio/out/ao_asio.c
@@ -570,7 +570,7 @@ static int pick_sample_type(struct asio_state *s)
         default:                wanted = native;           break;
         }
         if (wanted != native) {
-            MP_ERR(s, "ASIO driver uses %s but --ao-asio-sample-type forces "
+            MP_ERR(s, "ASIO driver uses %s but --asio-sample-type forces "
                    "a different type. The driver does not allow this.\n",
                    sample_type_name(native));
             return -1;
@@ -621,7 +621,7 @@ static int init(struct ao *ao)
     s->com_initialized = SUCCEEDED(hr);
 
     // Resolve driver name: --audio-device=asio/<name> stripped to <name>
-    // is in ao->device; --ao-asio-device is the explicit option.
+    // is in ao->device; --asio-device is the explicit option.
     const char *wanted = (ao->device && ao->device[0]) ? ao->device
                                                        : s->opt_device;
     wchar_t wname[ASIO_NAME_MAX] = {0};
